          }
        },
        "text_FDmm8T": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>The equinox marks the point in the year when day and night are equal in length, the pivot between summer's outward energy and winter's inward turn. In Traditional Chinese Medicine, late summer is considered a fifth season, one that lasts until the equinox arrives and asks us to shift from expansion toward reflection.</p><p>This is a season for noticing what's ripened: the seeds planted in spring, the growth of summer, and what has now come to be. It's a  welcome hibernation with your innermost circle, where you take a collective breath, and let the year settle into the much-needed quiet time.</p>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_font": "body",
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
