        "heading_dFGpNi": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "A Grounding Ritual for Autumn",
            "heading_size": "title-md",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "text_AECUt8": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Before the meal begins, step outside. Take a short walk, around the block or into the yard, and gather something along the way: a few fallen leaves, a handful of kindling, a late imperfect apple. The point isn't to collect, it's to notice.</p><p>Bring what you've gathered inside. If you have a fireplace or a fire pit, use the kindling to build the evening's fire. If you don't, let a few candles do the same work.</p><p>Before you sit down to eat, take a moment, just a breath or two, to look at the people around the table. No toast required, no words if you don't want them. Just the quiet acknowledgment of being together in this way.</p>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_font": "body",
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
